ed  their investment so years of this kind  of high spending and high borrowing was  already storing up problems for the  future and then when the current  president gotabaya rajapaksa came to  power in 2019  he made a couple of drastic decisions  that totally derailed the economy first  he followed through on his election  promise to bring in huge tax cuts but  those cuts meant the government suddenly  lost out on a lot of money about 25 of  its revenue the government stated that  the reason is that they were going to  give more incentives to people to  businesses to earn more and then develop  the economy and provide employment but  also the reason was that they were  facing a general election after the  presidential election a few months later  this was not something that was advised  it was not based on expert advice or  research it was a populist move the  other thing gotabaya rajapaksa did was  to force sri lanka's farmers to go  completely organic pretty much overnight  the government banned the use and import  of all chemical fertilizers no country  in the world goes into organic farming  100  at best developed countries like germany  have maybe 10 to 15 organic farming and  it was disastrous for sri lanka's  farmers who were suddenly producing much  less  it got so bad that sri lanka had to  start importing rice something it never  had to do before and food prices went  way up what it did was cause an  escalation in prices in the local market  for stable foods  that people were accustomed to getting  easily and cheaply and remember while  all this was going on over the last  couple of years there was also the  pandemic and all the problems that  caused the tourism industry took a  massive hit and tourism is usually one  of sri lanka's biggest earners and an  important source of foreign currency  so basically by the middle of last year  all of these problems were coming to a  head and it was already clear what kind  of financial trouble the country was in  but the government didn't really face up  to it analysts say the government is  like an ostrich with its head in the  sand not taking corrective action was  the huge governance and policy mistake  that was made i think which caused the  crash to be so much more severe than it  could have been so what happens now  well on the economic front sri lanka  desperately needs cash india has offered  up more money to help with fuel imports  china has also put more loans on the  table but the main hope is to secure a  big bailout from the imf  talks have started but a deal is still  months away at best these negotiations  have a lot of technical uh hoops to jump  over sri lanka really does have to show  that there is a legitimate stable  government that is able to  deliver on the undertakings given to the  imf and the creditors and that's a whole  other challenge because things really  aren't looking too stable on the  political front  president rajapaksa has now appointed a  new prime minister ranil gramasinghe  he's from an opposition party and has  had the job five times before  the idea is that he'll head up a new  unity government i.e one that brings  together lots of different political  parties to try to find a way out of the  crisis but so far the main opposition  parties are refusing to get on board  they want what the protesters want for  gotabaya to go  whatever happens the longer it all drags  out the more painful it's going to get  for the sri lankan people  some of those big infrastructure  projects that we talked about were  actually backed by china part of a mega  project known as the belt and road  initiative we've got an episode on that  which you can watch here  [Music]  you
 

